    "result_text": "Owners created with `sharesHousehold: true` now get a `householdId`, and the API returns it. The test suite passes: it contains one test, `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId`, and that test passes.\n\nMost of the rule was already there. The app already accepted `sharesHousehold` and returned 409 Conflict when another owner had the same last name and address without the flag. What was missing was storing and returning a shared ID.\n\n**How it works:** when a new owner is created with `sharesHousehold: true` and existing owners have the same last name and address (ignoring case and extra spaces):\n- If one of those owners already has a `householdId`, the new owner gets that same ID.\n- If none do, a new random ID (a UUID) is created and given to the new owner and the existing ones.\n\nSo when a second owner joins, the first owner gets the same ID too. Once set, a household ID doesn't change, and editing an owner later keeps it.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Database:** new `household_id` column on the `owners` table in all four schema scripts (H2, HSQLDB, MySQL, PostgreSQL).\n- **Model and mapping:** `Owner` has a `householdId` field. The mapper ignores it on input, so clients can't set it.\n- **Repository:** I replaced `findAddressesByLastName` with `findByLastNameIgnoringCase`, which returns whole owners, in all three repository versions (Spring Data JPA, JPA, JDBC). The JDBC queries and update statement now include `household_id`.\n- **Service:** new `ClinicService.joinHousehold(owner)`, which assigns the ID as described above. `ownerHouseholdExists` now uses the same matching code.\n- **Controller:** `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` calls `joinHousehold` when `sharesHousehold` is true.\n- **`openapi.yml`:** `householdId` is added to the `Owner` schema as a read-only field, and the `sharesHousehold` description explains the new behaviour.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- `householdId` is empty for owners who aren't in a shared household. That includes an owner created with `sharesHousehold: true` when nobody matches. They only get an ID when someone joins them later.\n- The tests only run against HSQLDB with Spring Data JPA. The plain JPA and JDBC versions compile but haven't been run, and neither have the MySQL and PostgreSQL schema changes.\n- The suite has no test for the case where nobody matches and none for the first owner getting the shared ID.",
